# emission.topic -- emission claims (`fn F(...) emits R:`), the guarantee
# the compiler certifies, and the diagnostics of a claim it does not.

@topic concept.emission-claims
title: Emission claims: `emits R`
summary: `fn F(...) emits R:` states that F yields one token of recognizer R; the compiler certifies it from F's declaration and code alone, for every permitted argument, or rejects it.
index: concepts proofs
alias: emits; emission claim; emitter; certified emitter; yield trace; output grammar; token emitter
keywords: emits, emitter, yield, trace, recognizer, dfa, token, certificate, certified, prefix, boundary, guard, domain, json, lexical
related: concept.recognizers concept.coroutines error.emit-rejected error.emit-boundary error.emit-unsupported

== WHAT IT IS
A clause of a function declaration, after its `errors`:

    fn json_string(text: str) -> unit errors IndexNotFound emits json_lex::json_string_strict:

It names one or more declared recognizers (`dfa NAME over ALPHABET:`, an
imported module's as `ns::NAME`). The compiler checks the claim when it
compiles the function and either certifies it or reports why not; a
function with a claim it cannot certify is a compile error. Nothing about
the function changes: it is called, returns, fails and yields exactly as
without the claim, the claim inserts no check at run time, needs no
coroutine of its own and grants no authority to write anything.

== THE GUARANTEE
For every argument tuple the function's declared parameter types permit:

  * every invocation that completes normally has yielded exactly one token
    of R, with nothing unread; and
  * every finite prefix of what an invocation yields -- before it
    completes, when it fails, or when its consumer stops resuming it -- is a
    viable prefix of R's language.

It does not say the function terminates, that the token has the value you
meant (a valid number of the wrong value is valid), or that a stream of
tokens is a structurally valid document: `emits json_text` proves the
lexical stream -- valid tokens with valid boundaries -- never that the
brackets match (that language is not regular).

== WHAT IS EMITTED
The outward yield trace: the values the function yields, and those its
ordinary calls yield (deep yields). A function's result is no symbol, and
neither is a coroutine root's final value, whatever its type: a source
claiming R may complete with any value (a count, a status, the -1 a
protocol uses for its end), which its consumer tells from a symbol with
std::coroutine::done?. A coroutine the function constructs is
another execution: its yields are its own, and the values the function
receives from it are ordinary values, of whatever the analysis knows of
them.

== CALLERS DO NOT MATTER
A claim is certified from the function's declared parameter types alone
-- an `int` is every Int, `type Digit = Int in 0..9` is 0..9 -- never from
the values its callers pass. A function no program calls is certified (or
rejected) exactly like one called everywhere, and the verdict is the same
in every program that imports it. Two honest repairs exist for an emitter
that is correct only for some values:

  * a declared integer domain: every caller must then prove its argument
    in the domain (a compile-time obligation of the call);
  * a guard: a call of a recognizer that never suspends, whose normal
    completion proves its argument is a symbol it takes -- the claim then
    says "fails, or emits a valid token".

== FAILURES AND HELPERS
A failure may come after a valid prefix: the claim allows it, and a caller
that handles the failure and goes on yielding is checked on its own
complete trace, from the states where the failure can occur. A helper
called from a claimed function needs no claim of its own; a helper that
has one is composed through its certificate. Two individually certified
token emitters called back to back do not make two tokens (`12` then `34`
is `1234`): a helper's claimed token must be ended by the next symbol of
its caller's grammar (error.emit-boundary).

== WHEN IT IS NOT PROVED
The compiler says "may": the facts it reasons with over-approximate what
runs. A rejected claim names the yield, the recognizer state and the
symbols; when the analysis lacks a fact (a value it cannot bound, a trait
operation, a budget) it says so instead (error.emit-unknown-value,
error.emit-unsupported, error.emit-budget). A concrete counterexample is a
run, never a compiler pass: audit/emission-claims/tools/witness.tcl runs the
function on candidate arguments and reports the first invalid trace it
finds -- and finding none proves nothing.

== RUNTIME COST
None: a certified emitter compiles to exactly the code it would without
its claim -- no recognizer runs, no table is loaded, no object or buffer is
allocated for the claim. A guard is the run-time check the program asked
for.

@example accept
import std::dfa
import std::coroutine

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digit_token over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> accept

type DigitValue = Int in 0..9

fn digit(d: DigitValue) -> unit emits digit_token:
    yield 48 + d
    unit

fn source() -> int:
    digit(7)
    -1

coroutine {step, first} = source()
first
@caption 48 + d is a digit for every d of the declared domain: certified, whoever calls it
@value 55
@end

@topic error.emit-rejected
title: EMIT-REJECTED: a yield the claimed recognizer may reject
summary: The analysis found that a yield of a claimed function may send a symbol the recognizer does not take in the state it is in: the claim cannot be certified.
index: errors proofs
alias: emitter rejected; invalid symbol; emission rejected
keywords: emits, emitter, yield, reject, recognizer, symbol, state, claim
related: concept.emission-claims concept.recognizers
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-REJECTED

== MEANING
For some argument the declaration permits, the yield may send a symbol no
transition of the recognizer's current state takes. The message names the
symbols and the state; a note locates the state's declaration. The
arguments are the declaration's: a program whose callers only pass safe
values does not make the claim true.

== WHAT TO DO
Make the emitter correct for every permitted argument: narrow the
parameter's declared type to an integer domain, branch on the value, or
guard it with a recognizer of one symbol. The witness tool
(audit/emission-claims/tools/witness.tcl) looks for a concrete argument
that produces an invalid trace.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digit_token over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> accept

type Nibble = Int in 0..15

fn digit(d: Nibble) -> unit emits digit_token:
    yield 48 + d
    unit

0
@caption 48 + d is ':' for d = 10: the declaration permits every Nibble, whoever calls it
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-REJECTED
@at 12:5
@end

@topic error.emit-beyond-token
title: EMIT-BEYOND-TOKEN: a yield the token ends before
summary: A claimed function may yield a symbol its recognizer completes before (`accept before`): the symbol would begin the next token, so the trace is more than one token.
index: errors proofs
alias: beyond the token; past the token
keywords: emits, accept before, lookahead, token, boundary, unread
related: concept.emission-claims error.emit-after-token
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BEYOND-TOKEN

== MEANING
`accept before N` completes a token leaving the last N symbols unread. A
claimed function yields exactly one token, so a symbol the token ends
before is not part of it.

== WHAT TO DO
Do not yield the follower: the caller that writes the next token (and
claims the larger grammar) yields it.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa number over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> integer
    integer:
        digit -> integer
        ',' -> accept before
        end -> accept

fn one_and_comma() -> unit emits number:
    yield 49
    yield 44
    unit

0
@caption a number ends before its ',': the ',' is the next token's
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BEYOND-TOKEN
@at 15:5
@end

@topic error.emit-after-token
title: EMIT-AFTER-TOKEN: a yield after the token is complete
summary: A claimed function may yield a symbol after its recognizer has completed the token on a symbol of its own.
index: errors proofs
alias: after the token
keywords: emits, token, complete, accept, after
related: concept.emission-claims error.emit-beyond-token
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-AFTER-TOKEN

== MEANING
The recognizer completed with nothing unread (an `accept` that takes the
symbol), so the token is whole; anything yielded after it is outside the
claimed language.

== WHAT TO DO
End the function after the token's last symbol, or claim a grammar of the
longer stream.

@example reject
import std::dfa

dfa quoted over ascii:
    start:
        '"' -> body
    body:
        'a'..'z' -> body
        '"' -> accept

fn quoted_then_space() -> unit emits quoted:
    yield 34
    yield 34
    yield 32
    unit

0
@caption the closing quotation mark completes the token: the space is after it
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-AFTER-TOKEN
@at 13:5
@end

@topic error.emit-incomplete
title: EMIT-INCOMPLETE: a claimed function may complete before its token does
summary: A normal completion of a claimed function may leave its recognizer in a state that rejects the end of the input: what it yielded is a prefix of a token, not a token.
index: errors proofs
alias: incomplete token; early completion; empty token
keywords: emits, end, complete, incomplete, return, prefix
related: concept.emission-claims
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-INCOMPLETE

== MEANING
Every normal completion -- the end of the body, every `return` -- must have
yielded a whole token: the recognizer must accept the end of the input
there. A prefix is allowed only before a failure or while the function is
still running.

A counted loop may run zero times unless its bounds prove otherwise: the
checker drops a loop's zero-iteration exit only where the facts before it
show that every start and end it may have put the start in the domain
(`from 0 to n` once `n >= 1` is known, a declared domain of positive
values). An entered loop still completes empty on a path its iterations
take without a symbol (a `break`, `continue` or `return` before the
yield).

== WHAT TO DO
Yield the rest of the token on every path that completes, or fail on the
path that cannot. For a loop that may run zero times, establish that it
runs before it (fail on the counts that would put nothing in it, or declare
the parameter's domain), or yield the token's first symbol before it.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digits over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> more
    more:
        digit -> more
        end -> accept

fn sevens(n: int) -> unit emits digits:
    loop i from 0 to n:
        yield 55
        continue
    unit

0
@caption for n <= 0 the loop runs zero times: no digit at all
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-INCOMPLETE
@at 12:33
@end

@example accept
import std::dfa
import std::coroutine

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digits over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> more
    more:
        digit -> more
        end -> accept

error NoDigits

fn sevens(n: int) -> unit errors NoDigits emits digits:
    if n < 1:
        fail NoDigits
    loop i from 0 to n:
        yield 55
        continue
    unit

fn source() -> int:
    sevens(3):
        on NoDigits:
            return -2
    -1

coroutine {step, first} = source()
first
@caption past the guard n >= 1, so the loop runs at least once: certified
@value 55
@end

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa signed over ascii:
    start:
        '-' -> minus
        digit -> integer
    minus:
        digit -> integer
    integer:
        digit -> integer
        end -> accept

fn minus_sign() -> unit emits signed:
    yield 45
    unit

0
@caption "-" alone is no number
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-INCOMPLETE
@at 15:31
@end

@topic error.emit-end-symbol
title: EMIT-END-SYMBOL: a yield that may send -1
summary: A claimed function may yield -1, which the recognizer protocol reserves for the end of the input: it is never a symbol.
index: errors proofs
alias: minus one; end of input yielded
keywords: emits, end, -1, symbol, protocol
related: concept.emission-claims concept.recognizers
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-END-SYMBOL

== MEANING
In std::dfa's protocol -1 is the end of the input. An emitter that yields
it would end its consumer's input in the middle of a stream.

== WHAT TO DO
Exclude -1 from the value: a branch, a domain, a guard.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digit_token over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> accept

fn previous(n: int) -> unit emits digit_token:
    if n >= 0 and n <= 10:
        yield n - 1
    else:
        yield 48
    unit

0
@caption n - 1 is -1 for n = 0
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-END-SYMBOL
@at 11:9
@end

@topic error.emit-not-symbol
title: EMIT-NOT-SYMBOL: a yield that sends no Int
summary: A claimed function yields a value that is not an Int (a String, a struct): a recognizer's symbols are Ints.
index: errors proofs
alias: not a symbol; string yielded
keywords: emits, yield, symbol, int, string, type
related: concept.emission-claims
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-NOT-SYMBOL

== MEANING
An emitter's yields are its symbols: Unicode scalar values, ASCII codes
or bytes, as Ints. A String is not a symbol (yield its characters'
std::char::scalar_value one at a time).

== WHAT TO DO
Yield Ints.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digit_token over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> accept

fn one() -> unit emits digit_token:
    yield "1"
    unit

0
@caption "1" is a String; 49 is the symbol
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-NOT-SYMBOL
@at 10:5
@end

@topic error.emit-unknown-value
title: EMIT-UNKNOWN-VALUE: a yield whose value the analysis cannot bound
summary: The analysis does not know a yielded value to be an Int of any bounded set (its static type is `any`): the claim is not proved, which is no finding that it is false.
index: errors proofs
alias: unknown value; any yielded
keywords: emits, yield, unknown, any, type, not proved
related: concept.emission-claims error.emit-unsupported
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-UNKNOWN-VALUE

== MEANING
The yielded value's type says nothing about it -- an element of a bare
`List`, a value of type `any` -- so no symbol set can be checked. This is
a limit of the facts, not a demonstrated error.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the type (`List[int]`, an integer domain) or check the value
before yielding it.

@example reject
import std::dfa
import std::list

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digit_token over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> accept

fn first(xs: List[any]) -> unit errors IndexNotFound emits digit_token:
    yield std::list::at(xs, 0)
    unit

0
@caption an element of a bare List is of type any
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-UNKNOWN-VALUE
@at 11:5
@end

@topic error.emit-byte-character
title: EMIT-BYTE-CHARACTER: a character's scalar value yielded to a byte recognizer
summary: A claimed function may yield a non-ASCII character's scalar value to a recognizer over bytes: a character is not a byte.
index: errors proofs
alias: character as byte; scalar value as byte
keywords: emits, bytes, character, scalar value, utf8, encoding
related: concept.emission-claims concept.recognizers
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BYTE-CHARACTER

== MEANING
A recognizer over bytes reads encoded text one byte at a time; a scalar
value above 127 is not one of its symbols, and yielding it is a mistake of
encoding, not of grammar.

== WHAT TO DO
Encode the character and yield its bytes, or claim a recognizer over
unicode.

@example reject
import std::dfa
import std::char
import std::str

dfa byte_token over bytes:
    start:
        0..255 -> accept

fn first_char(text: str) -> unit errors IndexNotFound emits byte_token:
    yield std::char::scalar_value(text.char_at(0))
    unit

0
@caption 'é' is the scalar value 233, which UTF-8 writes as two bytes
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BYTE-CHARACTER
@at 10:5
@end

@topic error.emit-boundary
title: EMIT-BOUNDARY: a symbol that would continue a helper's token
summary: After a call of a helper that claims to emit one whole token of a recognizer, the caller may yield a symbol that recognizer's token would take: the helper's token would absorb it.
index: errors proofs
alias: token boundary; glued tokens; adjacent tokens; missing separator
keywords: emits, boundary, token, helper, separator, adjacent, composition
related: concept.emission-claims error.emit-beyond-token
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BOUNDARY

== MEANING
Calling two certified number emitters back to back writes `12` and `34`,
which is the one number `1234`. A helper's claim is a promise that what it
yields is one token, so the symbol after it must end that token in the
caller's grammar -- whitespace or a separator, or the end.

== WHAT TO DO
Yield a separator the grammar ends the token before.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa number over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> integer
    integer:
        digit -> integer
        ' ' -> accept before
        end -> accept

dfa numbers over ascii:
    between:
        ' ' -> between
        digit -> call number then between
        end -> accept

type DigitValue = Int in 0..9

fn digit(d: DigitValue) -> unit emits number:
    yield 48 + d
    unit

fn glued() -> unit emits numbers:
    digit(1)
    digit(2)
    unit

0
@caption "12" is one number, not the two digit wrote
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BOUNDARY
@at 27:5
@end

@topic error.emit-unsupported
title: EMIT-UNSUPPORTED: code the emission checker does not follow
summary: A claimed function's trace depends on code the checker does not follow (a trait operation, a context-trait implementation, an unknown form): not proved, which is no finding that it is false.
index: errors proofs
alias: unsupported; not modelled; trait operation in an emitter
keywords: emits, trait, unsupported, not proved, context trait, implementation
related: concept.emission-claims error.emit-unknown-value error.emit-budget
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
A trait operation's implementation is chosen for the program it is in, so
a claim that read it would be a property of that program, not of the
function. The checker refuses to certify what it does not follow -- never
does it read an unknown operation as yielding nothing. This is a limit of
the analysis, not a demonstrated error.

== WHAT TO DO
Call a concrete function instead (the trait operation's implementation, by
name), or move the output into a helper that does not depend on the trait.

@example reject
import std::dfa

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa digit_token over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> accept

trait Coded:
    fn code(self: Coded) -> int

struct Seven:
    unused: int

fn code(s: Seven) -> int:
    55

fn emit(c: Coded) -> unit emits digit_token:
    yield c.code()
    unit

fn source() -> int:
    emit(Seven {unused: 0})
    -1

coroutine {step, first} = source()
first
@caption c.code() is the witness's, chosen where emit is called
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-UNSUPPORTED
@at 18:33
@end

@topic error.emit-budget
title: EMIT-BUDGET: the emission analysis exhausted its budget
summary: The analysis of a claim took more fixpoint rounds, summaries or recognizer products than its budget allows: not proved, never proved.
index: errors proofs
alias: budget; analysis limit
keywords: emits, budget, fixpoint, rounds, recursion, limit, not proved
related: concept.emission-claims error.emit-unsupported
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-BUDGET

== MEANING
Recursion and loops are analyzed to a fixpoint whose convergence is
checked: 60 rounds, 40,000 summaries of helpers per compilation, 20,000
states of a certificate's product with a caller's grammar. A claim whose
analysis needs more is rejected as not proved, and nothing it reached is
reused. This is a limit of the analysis, not a demonstrated error.

== WHAT TO DO
Split the emitter, or bound what changes between recursive calls (an
argument whose facts grow every round is widened, but a long chain of
distinct helpers is not).

@topic error.emit-recognizer
title: EMIT-RECOGNIZER: a claim that names no recognizer
summary: An `emits` clause names something that is not a visible declared recognizer, or names one twice.
index: errors proofs
alias: emits names no recognizer; emits names a function; not a recognizer
keywords: emits, recognizer, dfa, unknown, name, claim, import
related: concept.emission-claims concept.recognizers
code: CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-RECOGNIZER

== MEANING
A claim names a recognizer declared with `dfa NAME over ALPHABET:` before
the function (an imported module's as `ns::NAME`, which needs its
`import`, like any qualified name). An ordinary function is no recognizer,
even one that yields.

== WHAT TO DO
Name the recognizer, import its module, or declare it before the function.

@example reject
import std::dfa

fn digit() -> unit emits nothing_declared:
    yield 49
    unit

0
@caption no recognizer of that name is declared
@error CORE SEMANTIC EMIT-RECOGNIZER
@at 3:26
@end
